RECORD OF PROCEEDINGS FOR THE GOVERNING BODY
OF THE CITY OF CHEYENNE

Office of City Clerk

March 8, 2017

The Governing Body of the City of Cheyenne convened in a special meeting on this date
beginning at 6:05 p.m. in City Council Chambers for the purpose of collective labor bargaining
with the Cheyenne Professional Fire Fighters, International Association of Fire Fighters, Local
No. 279.  Present were:  MAYOR - Marian J. Orr; COUNCIL MEMBERS – Rocky Case, Bryan
Cook, Richard Johnson, Pete Laybourn, Mike Luna, Scott Roybal (arriving at 7:30 p.m.) and Dicky
Shanor.  Absent: Dr. Mark Rinne and Jeff White.  Also present were:  Kylie Soden, Deputy City
Clerk; Sylvia Hackl, City Attorney; Alessandra McCoy, Assistant City Attorney, Lois Huff, City
Treasurer, and Tom Schingle, Interim Chief, Fire and Rescue Department.  The pledge of
allegiance was recited.

Mayor Orr advised the meeting would be audio recorded, and stated that Wyoming State
Statute §16-4-405(a)(x) of the Public Meetings Act allows the governing body to meet in executive
session to consider or tender offers regarding wages, salaries, or other terms and conditions of
employment during negotiations with the fire fighters union and that any motion approved by the
governing body to move into executive session during the special meetings would be pursuant to
this section of statute.  Mayor Orr further advised if the governing body met in executive session,
they would be meeting in the City Council Administrative office, and that Fire Union
representatives were welcome to use Room 104 for the purpose of meeting to caucus.  She stated
that spokespersons for the City are herself and Dicky Shanor, and Jonathan Bertsch and Andrew
Dykeshorn are the spokespersons for the Union.  She noted that any collective labor agreement
that may be reached must be approved by the governing body with the agreement placed on the
City Council agenda for approval.

Mayor Orr stated, with agreement from the Union, the “$20/hr Analysis” handout that was
distributed by the City at the March 6th meeting had been provided to the media.  Mr. Bertsch
briefly outlined modifications proposed by the Union, which were in addition to their last
counteroffers, to the following sections, or portions thereof: Section 15, Company Strength;
Section 22, Classification of Employment and Basic Wages; Section 27, Wellness/Fitness
Program, and Section 33, Uniforms.  Upon inquiry, Mr. Bertsch provided clarifications on Sections
27 and 15.  Mr. Shanor moved to meet in executive session, seconded by Mr. Luna.  Motion
carried.  Voting “yes” -- all members of the governing body present.  [City Clerk note: Audio
recording of the meeting was stopped; executive session convened at 6:20 p.m. in the City Council
Administrative office]

Following closure of the executive session, Mayor Orr reconvened the special meeting at
7:54 p.m. and noted that Mr. Roybal had joined the meeting [City Clerk note: Mr. Roybal joined
executive session at 7:30 p.m.].  She expressed the importance of predictability of overtime,
sustainability of services and management rights.  Mr. Shanor presented the City’s counteroffer
on the following sections, or portions thereof: Section 4, Term of Agreement; Section 17,
```

### Page 2 of 2

```text
Severance Pay; Section 22, Classification of Employment and Basic Wages; Section 23, Overtime;
Section 27, Wellness/Fitness Program; Section 29, Sick Leave, and Section 33, Uniforms.  Mayor
Orr recessed the meeting at 8:06 p.m. to allow Union representatives to caucus, and Mr. Shanor
moved to meet in executive session, seconded by Mr. Roybal.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” -- all
members of the governing body present.  [City Clerk note: Audio recording of the meeting was
stopped; executive session convened at 8:10 p.m. in the City Council Administrative office]

Following closure of the executive session, Mayor Orr reconvened the special meeting at
8:32 p.m.  Mr. Bertsch reviewed dates for future special meetings that the Union is available to
meet.  Upon inquiry, Mr. Shanor clarified information on the City’s proposal for Section 33,
Uniforms, stating the City has no objection to the Union’s most recent offer, with the City’s
proposed changes to subsection C.  Mr. Bertsch advised that the Union has no objection to Section
33, and presented and explained counteroffers on the following sections, or portions thereof:
Section 22, Classification of Employment and Basic Wages; Section 23, Overtime, and Section
29, Sick Leave.

Mayor Orr stated the next special meeting would be held on March 10, 2017.  She stated
the purpose of the special meeting had been met, and there being no other business previously
noted for the special meeting, adjourned the meeting at 8:40 p.m.

Submitted by,

Kylie Soden

Deputy City Clerk

This notice is available in alternative, accessible formats upon request.

Published:
Wyoming Tribune-Eagle

March 23, 2017
```
